{"kind":"request","text":"Review and incorporate: design note, pointing and claims in packages.\n\nBranch planner/pointing-claims-packages-20261027, head 77d0c5520a3e081384efbd96e7717617f2983332, base main 6f47bf98fa09ac0f3957ac9f6cc3ae21b2333b1e, pushed. One path: notes/2026-10-27-pointing-and-claims.md (new). It changes no landed document. The doc-links check passes (712 links, 0 broken) and the commit-message check passes.\n\nDirection (Hugh, 2026-10-27): the package implementation is early, so adjust the application-package structure to the boundary-objects view: concrete things with nebulous significance; strong activity rules with a flexible interface; a room supports both pointing at things and claims of consensus; thick interfaces (the MCP Apps views) on the person's side of the boundary between people and agents.\n\nWhat the note proposes:\n1. A commons package that every room installs: `thing` items (a label and unverified locators, no final state); statements as comment entries (`say`); `claim` items with named parties, kinds agree, dispute, close and same, and signatures, where the claim takes effect in the entry of the last signature. Acts point, locate, say, withdraw-statement, claim, sign, withdraw-claim and adopt. Section 3.\n2. How a package reads the commons: an `about` field of facts of kind `point`; a `consent` rule on party slots (signer, claim, declared) that the validator checks, so a package enters the common record only through a claim by the parties it binds or through an authority one party already holds. Section 4.\n3. Presentation packages adopted by each member (`adopt`), so each party reads the record under its own projections and act descriptions. The MCP Apps views are the thick side and the generic tools the thin side of one declared projection. Section 5.\n4. An item's final state ends the item and never a party's matter; a party closes its own part with a `close` claim. Section 6.\n5. Five witnesses W1 to W5 for every package that connects to the commons, with cases 29 and 30 as the exit test. Section 7.\n6. Statements that change (section 8), slices PC1 to PC4 (section 9, not filed), bounds to measure (section 10), and the use cases and dap-spike directions served (section 11).\n\nOpen for the checker: the encoding of \"every party signed\" (a conditional effect over two lists, or a rule expression) and of the presented agreeing entry; the bound of 100 things for each room; whether statements as comment entries need a projection of their own.\n\nAsked of the planner: file a checker review at this head; revise for findings with child commits on the branch; land as a planner head; then amend the self-contained applications note (sections 4 and 6) and the packages note (sections 2 and 3) as section 8 lists, and add the roadmap row. Do not file PC1 to PC4 until Hugh orders it. The companion catalogue head is on branch planner/use-cases-pointing-20261027, filed as its own request on the same date.","body":{"conditions":"notes/2026-10-27-pointing-and-claims.md lands on main after checker approval, with its status line matching main; this request files no implementation request.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}